## Final Judgement
## Summary of Key Findings

The evidence overwhelmingly and unambiguously refutes the claim. The World Bank and IMF were established at the Bretton Woods Conference in July 1944, with Harry Dexter White, John Maynard Keynes, and Henry Morgenthau recognized as their principal architects. Dr. Jose Rizal was executed in 1896—nearly 50 years before these institutions existed—making it physically impossible for him to have founded them. Ferdinand Marcos did not become President of the Philippines until 1965, two decades after the World Bank and IMF were already operational. No credible historical or institutional record lists either Marcos or Rizal among the founders of these institutions. The only "source" for the claim appears to be a conspiracy theory document hosted on Scribd.

## Missing Information

None. The evidence is comprehensive and conclusive. Multiple authoritative sources (including the World Bank's own official documents, the Bretton Woods Project, and the European Parliament) consistently confirm the timeline and founders, and the impossibility of the claim is established by basic biographical facts.

## Conclusion

The claim is explicitly and clearly refuted by overwhelming evidence. Both individuals named in the claim could not have founded the World Bank and IMF: Rizal died approximately 48 years before these institutions were created, and Marcos became president approximately 20 years after they were already established. The actual founders were American and British economists and officials at the 1944 Bretton Woods Conference. The claim appears to originate from a conspiracy theory document with no credible basis.

`refuted`

